  "step_3": "The decision reason requires calculating an aspiration level as the mean of all outcomes (-3000) and then comparing the probabilities of outcomes that exceed this level (i.e., > -3000). Only the outcome 0 qualifies, so the comparison reduces to the probability of 0: 55% in A vs. 10% in B. While this leads to the same preference as the individual's choice (A), the verbal report does not mention or imply such a calculation. The individual does not reference an aspiration level, nor do they describe evaluating outcomes relative to -3000. Instead, they focus on avoiding loss altogether and the high probability of losing in B, treating 0 as a desirable outcome (no loss), not as a deviation from a computed reference point. The reasoning is loss-aversion-based and probability-focused, but not structured around deviations from an aspiration level computed as a mean of all outcomes. There is no evidence of the specific cognitive operation (mean of outcomes, comparison of deviations) that defines the reason. Therefore, while the direction of choice aligns, the process described does not match the reason's mechanism.",
  "final_assessment_score": 20
}

